NORMALITY AS A MODEL — probed 2026-10-15, NOT adopted. Three findings, one blocker.

THE QUESTION. Theorem 10.11 takes four hypotheses that together say "N is a normal
subgroup of A_n": inN(identityFn), closure under products, closure under inverses,
and the conjugation condition. Could one `model` declaration replace them?

FINDING 1 — THE PERMUTATIONS DO MODEL `group`, cleanly. See
permutations-model-group.b4m: a GUARDED model onto `Perm` (= `Fn where invertible`),
nominating identityInvertible / composeInvertible / inverseInvertible as the
membership dischargers, and discharging the three group axioms with composeAssoc /
composeIdentityLeft / inverseLeft. `group.invInvolution` then transfers, relativized
to `invertible`. Landed first try. This piece is worth having on its own merits —
nothing in std currently models group onto permutations, so the whole group corpus is
unavailable to permutation files.

FINDING 2 — A SCHEMA PARAMETER CANNOT BE A MODEL TARGET. See
schema-param-cannot-be-a-model-target.b4m. A model needs a file-level predicated sort
(`sort SubN = Fn where inN`), and `inN` exists only inside the schema's own scope:

    error: reference not found: 'inN'

So "N is a normal subgroup" cannot become a model declaration IN THE CAPSTONE. It can
only be declared by a CONSUMER, which already has a concrete predicate. The capstone
therefore keeps its hypotheses either way; a model would move the boilerplate from the
theorem's statement to each consumer's file, not remove it.

FINDING 3 — THE BLOCKER, and it is the one worth writing down. We want to axiomatize
what normality needs, but THOSE AXIOMS CARRY AN OVERLOAD REQUIREMENT WHENEVER THE BASE
SORT IS NOT UNIVERSAL.

`std/subgroup.b4m` is written against a universal base: `sort Sub = Grp where
inSubgroup` has ONE guard, and its proofs' `[by predicate ...]` steps expect exactly
that one. Permutations are not universal — `Perm = Fn where invertible` — so the target
sort `EvenPerm = Perm where inAlternating` carries TWO guards, and every transferred
binder relativizes to the CONJUNCTION. The mismatch lands inside the source file's own
proof (see alternating-as-a-subgroup.b4m):

    std/subgroup.b4m:152:8: error: AlternatingIsASubgroup@subgroupClosedDivide:
      step claims 'inAlternating(g)' but the hypothesis derives
      'inAlternating(g) and invertible(g)'

GUIDE.md documents the conjunction injection for multi-qualifier sorts, so this is the
mechanism working as specified — the gap is that a source theory's proofs are written
against the arity of ITS OWN guard, and a refined-base target changes that arity.

WHAT THAT MEANS FOR THE DESIGN. An abstract theory meant to be modeled onto a REFINED
base sort has to be stated so its membership obligations are overload-tolerant: either
every guard-consuming step takes the guard as an explicit premise rather than through
`[by predicate]`, or the model mechanism learns to project a conjunction onto the
single guard the source names. The second is the real fix and is engine work.

Related: [[required-on-model-primitive]] records model_guarded* blocked on a different
missing primitive ("required on model"); this is a third guarded-model gap, distinct
from both that and the @-projection route in tests/cases/model_subgroup_transfer.b4m
(which works precisely because its base sort IS universal).

STATUS. Finding 1 is adoptable now and would be a real addition to std. Findings 2 and
3 say the capstone's hypotheses stay. Not pursuing further without a decision on the
overload question.
